The analytics service (Phase 5B, part one). `services/analytics.py` answers the four questions the app has been recording answers to for four phases without showing any of them back - is ingestion keeping up, how are tickets flowing, what are the models costing, and is the approval gate being used - by aggregating `IngestionStats`, `EventTicket`, `LLMUsageRecord`, `AgentRun` and `AgentToolCall`. It adds no model, no counter and no writer of any kind (rule D1), and it calls no model of the other sort either (rule D7). Every function takes `user` and aggregates over a queryset restricted to them, following the parent for records that hang off a ticket (rule D2): a count is an oracle, and a cost chart built from unrestricted usage records is a ticket-existence oracle with a price attached. Every query is bounded by an explicit time window, and each panel's window is clamped further to the retention of the table it reads, so a ninety-day chart over a thirty-day retention reports thirty days rather than drawing sixty days of zeroes it invented. Configured by a new `dashboard` block, which is the first in the app that is on by default.

The analytics dashboard (Phase 5B, part two). **Apps → Event Tracker → Analytics** draws nine panels over what the app has been recording for four phases: the ingestion funnel and why messages were dropped, tickets opened and closed with the open ones split by severity and a median time to close, model cost by day and by model with a failure rate, and agent runs and tool-call decisions. Every chart is an `EChartsPanel` from Nautobot's public UI API, and a time-window control bounded by `max_window_days` sits above them. A panel whose window runs past the retention of the table it reads reports the range it actually covered, so a ninety-day chart over a thirty-day retention says "last 30 days (retention; 90 requested)" rather than drawing sixty days of zeroes. A panel that exceeds `query_timeout_seconds` says so and the rest of the page still renders. On by default; setting `dashboard.enabled` to `False` removes the route and the menu item rather than leaving a page that returns 404.
